WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.220
Matthew: Good afternoon!

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.410
I'm Matthew Spalding, Associate Vice-President
in Dean of Educational Programs for Hillsdale

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.120
College here in Washington, D.C.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.120
Welcome!

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.080
We're at Allan P. Kirby Center for Constitutional
Studies &amp; Citizenship.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.660
Hillsdale College was founded in 1844 by men
and women believing that the diffusion of

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.720
learning is essential to the perpetuity of
the blessings of civil and religious liberty.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.519
We take learning in those liberties very seriously.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:34.390
It was George Washington in his great 1790
letter to the Jewish Congregation in Newport,

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:40.020
Rhode Island, the Jewish people being one
of the most persecuted peoples in history,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.530
who reminded us that Americans enjoy their
religious liberty not as a matter of toleration

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.230
but as an exercise of inherent right found
up in man's very nature.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:56.270
In rapidly changing times like these, we are
reminded how important it is to secure and

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.530
strengthen our first freedom.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.850
We're very pleased to have our speaker with
us here today.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.190
He'll be introduced by one of our students.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.160
Sarah Ankin is from Seymour, Indiana.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.570
She's a junior at Hillsdale College where
she is a Politics and Math Double Major.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:14.120
She is a George Washington Fellow and currently
interning at the Heritage Foundation.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.190
Sarah.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:20.520
Sarah: Thank you, Dr. Spalding.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.050
Good afternoon ladies and gentlemen and welcome.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.450
As a born and raised Hoosier, I am very interested
in our speaker's talk this afternoon on the

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.450
War in Indiana.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:35.830
Me and my friends, family members, classmates
and professors fiercely debated the controversy

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:41.130
over Indiana's Religious Freedom and Restoration
Act, and left some of us irritably divided.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:46.140
It's my pleasure to introduce Mr. David French
who explained this issue and outlined what

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.380
it means for religious liberty in Indiana
and our country.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.820
David French is an attorney, writer and veteran
of the Iraq war.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.780
He's a graduate of Harvard Law School, the
past president of the Foundation for Individual

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.240
Rights in Education, and former lecturer at
Cornell Law School.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.790
His legal work defending religious liberty
on college campuses helped inspired the 2014

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:12.020
hit movie, God's Not Dead.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:16.820
A writer with national review, Mr. French
is also the author and co-author of several

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.990
book including A Season for Justice, Defending
the Rights of the Christian home, church and

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:30.090
school, and Rise of Isis: A Threat We Can't
Ignore, a New York Times number one bestseller.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:36.670
He contributes regularly to Patheos and his
work has also appeared in the Washington Post,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.410
Washington Times and Human Events.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.650
French has appeared on ABC World News Tonight,
CNN Newsroom and Special Report with Brit

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:45.650
Hume.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.570
A regular guest on radio talk show programs,
French has been interviewed with National

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:55.840
Public Radio and by numerous hosts including
Michael Melved, Hugh Hewitt and Dennis Prager.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.440
Mr. French is a major of the US Army Reserve.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.350
In 2007, he deployed to Iraq as a Squadron
Judge Advocate for the Second Squadron, Third

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.380
Armored Cavalry Regiment, and he was awarded
the Bronze Star at the conclusion of his tour.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.750
He lives in Columbia, Tennessee with his wife,
Nancy, and their three children.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.400
Please join me in welcoming Mr. David French.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.440
David French: Thank you very much.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.170
First, I want to thank the Kirby Center in
Hillsdale for inviting me.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:33.381
It's a real honor to speak on this topic,
especially to folks in the audience, who just

00:03:33.381 --> 00:03:38.370
looking at the audience, I know could deliver
an extended and better a lecture on this subject

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.300
than I can.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:46.850
I want to begin where most discussions of
important cultural issue should begin and

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:51.490
that is with a Michael Bay and Jerry Bruckheimer
movie.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.040
Some of you may remember this one, a classic,
called Armageddon.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:02.420
Armageddon, if you forget the premise, was
that the world discovers that it is about

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.730
to end ... I think that's the premise for
most Michael Bay and Jerry Bruckheimer movies,

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:13.380
and it's going to end at the hands of a gigantic
asteroid or meteorite that is hurdling towards

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:18.709
the earth and will create a cataclysmic extinction-level
event.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:25.660
The only way to stop it is to send a group
of deep core drillers, oil wildcatters out

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.880
to space led, of course, by Bruce Willis to
drill through the asteroid, plant a nuclear

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.990
bomb, detonate the asteroid, and come home
to safety and heroes' welcome.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.600
Fantastic film.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.720
The reason why it applies is I was trying
to think of an analogy for how the culture

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.300
war of a religious liberty is shaking out
and I couldn't think of a better analogy than

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.820
the process of mining.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.640
The most vivid movie I can ever remember about
mining was Armageddon.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:01.590
If you remember it and no doubt you do, they
landed on an asteroid in horrific conditions

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:06.150
after enduring many troubles, and they began
the process of mining.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.350
They feel like their home-free.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:14.699
They are cutting through soft rock for minutes,
for hours, they're on schedule.

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:16.160
Then what happens?

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.250
They hit some kind of hard space rock.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:25.470
Their drill bits break, chaos occurs, people
die, and at the very end of it, Bruce Willis

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.730
has to sacrifice himself to save the world.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.530
What does it have to do with religious liberty?

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:41.850
I would submit to you that for some time the
Left in making progress, in damaging religious

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:47.570
liberty in this country has been drilling
through an awful lot of soft rock.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:54.669
It has been going through a public that is
largely unengaged to church, largely uninformed

00:05:54.669 --> 00:06:01.650
about their reason for their values, heavily
swayed by pop culture, and ready to receive

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:07.919
a message that is simple, that is compelling,
and that casts a certain group of people in

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:12.320
white hats and a certain group of people in
black hats; easily susceptible to things like

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:20.050
race analogies; easily susceptible to changes
in perception by viewing, for example, sitcoms.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:23.699
How often has anyone heard that one of the
most important developments for gay rights

00:06:23.699 --> 00:06:26.870
in the United States was the sitcom, Will
and Grace?

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:35.100
I would submit to you that if a sitcom is
changing your world view, you are soft rock.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.389
Where I live in the south, a soft rock is
lime stone.

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:44.440
Lime stone crumbles easily, drilled through
easily, but then sometimes when you're drilling,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.370
you hit granite.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:48.110
You hit hard rock.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:53.800
I believe that the reason for the intensity
of the Battle of Indiana, which is only one

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:59.990
of many battles, is because the soft rock
has been stripped away and the cultural Left

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:06.970
is now encountering the granite of the base
of the cultural right, and it is not making

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.950
progress.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:15.410
I could say, if there would be one subtitle
to this talk it would be "Against Despair".

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:21.740
The soft rock has been stripped away, the
granite remains, and now they are the ones

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:29.940
who are carrying the baggage of an easily
swayed sitcom-watching, sitcom-influenced

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:37.850
population, whereas we are now increasingly
pairing down to a base million strong of people

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:41.270
who are not willing to budge on core issues.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.200
Not willing to budge at all, and in fact,
they're willing to push back and push back

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.200
decisively.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:53.820
I didn't look at Indiana the way many people
looked at Indiana as a defeat for cultural

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:54.820
conservatism.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.370
Unquestionably, there was a element of political
defeat.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:06.740
Governor Pence and Indiana Republicans revised
the statute post-haste to try to deal with

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:12.430
popular objections that really had very little
basis in law or fact.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.490
The Left declared victory.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.370
They'd won a political battle there.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:20.259
They had won what appeared to them to be a
PR battle, but there were deeper realities

00:08:20.259 --> 00:08:24.130
there that I'm going to talk about.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:29.330
Here are four truths that I think are emerging
as a result of the Battle of Indiana and many

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:33.669
other battles that have gone before and the
battles that will come after.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:41.279
First, this is not a battle between "gay rights
and religious liberty"; it's just not.

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:46.889
It is a battle between the sexual revolution
in orthodox Christianity.

00:08:46.889 --> 00:08:54.709
I would also include other religions as well,
Judaism, Islam, but the focus of the battle,

00:08:54.709 --> 00:08:57.009
the focus is on the Christian community.

00:08:57.009 --> 00:09:02.720
It's a battle of sexual revolution versus
orthodox Christianity.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:09.660
Second, because increasingly, Christian thinkers
and the larger Christian public is realizing

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:16.779
that orthodox denominations and orthodox sectors,
small, low, of course when I'm using the term

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:24.569
orthodox, are not looking to revise or amend
or change their policies and their practices

00:09:24.569 --> 00:09:28.410
and their theology surrounding sexual revolution
issues.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.410
They're not.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:35.929
Name one major American denomination that
you would consider evangelical or orthodox

00:09:35.929 --> 00:09:40.811
that is thinking about revising, for example,
or seriously debating revising whether or

00:09:40.811 --> 00:09:45.129
not it's going to recognize same sex marriages;
or revising whether or not it's going to support

00:09:45.129 --> 00:09:46.129
abortion on demand.

00:09:46.129 --> 00:09:47.129
There's not one.

00:09:47.129 --> 00:09:49.119
It's not on the horizon.

00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:56.480
Third, and part because of this, rather than
retreating quietly, cultural conservatism

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:01.339
is showing an enormous amount of strength
at the grassroots level.

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:07.329
Not at the pop culture level, not in the level
of the mainstream media, not in major coastal

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:11.190
cities, but at the grassroots levels it is
showing an enormous amount of strength.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:17.239
Fourth, and this is vitally important as well,
the intellectual backbone of the conservative

00:10:17.239 --> 00:10:22.050
movement in the United States is not wavering
on these key issues.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:27.339
From the folks at the highest purchase, for
example, Ross Douthat at the New York Times,

00:10:27.339 --> 00:10:33.639
the folks who are writing broadly about it
at multiple other publications, you were seeing

00:10:33.639 --> 00:10:38.540
an enormous amount of unanimity that's what
is happening, for example, in Indiana, what

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:43.709
has happened to Hobby Lobby, what has happened
to Chick-fil-A, what has happened to Brendan

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:51.009
Eich, a large degree of consensus amongst
significant thinkers in the conservative movement

00:10:51.009 --> 00:10:56.170
and in the Christian movement, more broadly,
that this is wrong, this is bad.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.949
You'll have a lot of divergent as to whether
or not doom is upon us.

00:11:00.949 --> 00:11:04.939
There's I think an emerging consensus often
that doom is upon us.

00:11:04.939 --> 00:11:08.110
There's a word that Rod Dryer has mentioned
several ... or phrase he's mentioned is the

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.559
Benedict Option.

00:11:09.559 --> 00:11:13.570
A strategic retreat from the culture.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.269
I disagree with that and I'll discuss that
a little bit.

00:11:16.269 --> 00:11:19.589
I believe those four principles are in play.

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:25.410
The good news means, with those four principles
in play, defeat for those who are interested

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:30.689
in religious liberty and advocate for religious
liberty is not imminent.

00:11:30.689 --> 00:11:35.959
Here's the bad news, neither is victory.

00:11:35.959 --> 00:11:41.079
I hesitate to use war analogies because what
we have here, thankfully in the United States,

00:11:41.079 --> 00:11:44.800
is not religious war, it's not.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:50.709
I especially hesitate war analogies and persecution
analogies given the fact that what's happening

00:11:50.709 --> 00:11:56.139
overseas is horrifying on a scale that dwarfs
anything that we're experiencing as a result

00:11:56.139 --> 00:12:01.940
of battles like Indiana or fights like Hobby
Lobby endured.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:04.220
You can't really compare those two.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.579
Having said that, I'm going to go ahead and
use a war analogy because they can be vivid

00:12:08.579 --> 00:12:09.670
and instructive.

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:15.670
I think what we're beginning to go into is
a period of trench warfare, of essential stalemate

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:23.600
where the two bases clash with each other
again and again with not necessarily persuading

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:29.790
each other at stake, but persuading the softer
middle, that limestone that I talked about

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:33.059
to mix the metaphors.

00:12:33.059 --> 00:12:37.720
Before I dive deeper into those four truths,
let me just set the stage for this battle

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:38.879
of Indiana.

00:12:38.879 --> 00:12:44.770
For almost everyone in here, this is going
to be old hat, so you can tune out, you can

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:49.540
check your iPhone quickly and surreptitiously
if you want.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:55.429
Let's just get some truth into this debate
about Indiana, specifically.

00:12:55.429 --> 00:13:01.350
Indiana's law, its version of the Religious
Freedom Restoration Act, was not in any way

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:09.199
shape or form an attempt to reimpose Jim Crow,
nor was it an attempt to create a Jim Crow-like

00:13:09.199 --> 00:13:12.809
regime for gay and lesbian citizens of Indiana.

00:13:12.809 --> 00:13:14.519
It was not.

00:13:14.519 --> 00:13:19.549
It was a law very much like a law that existed
that time in 19 other states and the United

00:13:19.549 --> 00:13:24.800
States government that said that you can't
quite simply, that if there's a substantial

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:30.899
burden on your religious liberty, that burden
cannot be overcome by a state action unless

00:13:30.899 --> 00:13:36.999
it's justified by compelling government interest,
accomplished with the least restrictive means.

00:13:36.999 --> 00:13:38.019
That's a legal test.

00:13:38.019 --> 00:13:43.269
It established a legal test, nothing more,
nothing less.

00:13:43.269 --> 00:13:49.230
It just so happen to establish a legal test
that did not fall out of the clear blue sky.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:55.809
It is a legal test that has existed for generations
in American law, in generations applied specifically

00:13:55.809 --> 00:13:57.499
to religious liberty.

00:13:57.499 --> 00:14:02.510
It's a legal test that existed simultaneously,
and this is very important to note, with the

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:08.100
explosive growth of non-discrimination statues
in the United States.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:12.619
That legal test comes from the Supreme Court
of the United States, which at the same time

00:14:12.619 --> 00:14:19.829
that the court was expanding its protection,
and was pioneering constitutional doctrine,

00:14:19.829 --> 00:14:22.720
and upholding protections against race discrimination.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:28.689
It was also implementing a test for the free
exercise clause of the first amendment that

00:14:28.689 --> 00:14:33.490
was the compelling governmental interest test.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:36.189
Those two legal developments existed side
by side.

00:14:36.189 --> 00:14:40.310
On the one hand, if you're going to infringe
on religious liberty, you had to justify it

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:43.420
with a compelling governmental interest, you'd
at least restate your means.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:48.980
On the other hand, we were in the United States
enacting huge numbers of public accommodation

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:53.529
statutes, non-discrimination statutes at the
federal and at the state levels.

00:14:53.529 --> 00:15:01.929
In fact, the Supreme Court in 1968 in a footnote
talked about a conflict, claimed conflict,

00:15:01.929 --> 00:15:07.529
between religious liberty and a public accommodation
statute that prohibited discrimination on

00:15:07.529 --> 00:15:16.869
the basis of race, and declared in that case,
Newman versus Piggie Park Enterprises, 1968,

00:15:16.869 --> 00:15:23.509
that a business owner's claim, they're decision
to exclude African-Americans from their business

00:15:23.509 --> 00:15:28.059
was justified by their faith was "patently
frivolous".

00:15:28.059 --> 00:15:33.059
That's the actual phrase that the Supreme
Court used, "patently frivolous".

00:15:33.059 --> 00:15:39.129
If anyone says that the compelling governmental
interest standard supports and protects Jim

00:15:39.129 --> 00:15:42.819
Crow, ask an answer.

00:15:42.819 --> 00:15:43.819
Ask an answer.

00:15:43.819 --> 00:15:45.199
What was at stake in Indiana?

00:15:45.199 --> 00:15:51.529
It was not the ability to engage in class-based
discrimination, but rather the creation of

00:15:51.529 --> 00:15:59.720
a legal test that on a case by case basis
could potentially protect religious liberty

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:07.749
in circumstances that are not acceptable to
one or other side of the political spectrum.

00:16:07.749 --> 00:16:12.629
In the example of Indiana which caused an
enormous amount of controversy, that test

00:16:12.629 --> 00:16:21.649
would involve, say for example a decision
to not provide a wedding ... Let me be precise

00:16:21.649 --> 00:16:29.040
of Indiana, a pizza not provide a pizza to
a wedding reception for a same sex marriage

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:35.379
even though, for example, that pizza owner
would be happy to provide pizza to a wedding

00:16:35.379 --> 00:16:38.609
of a gay man if the man were marrying a woman.

00:16:38.609 --> 00:16:40.059
There was not class-based discrimination.

00:16:40.059 --> 00:16:48.439
It was an unwillingness to participate or
to cooperate and to facilitate a ceremony

00:16:48.439 --> 00:16:51.509
that the pizzeria owner found to be objectionable.

00:16:51.509 --> 00:16:57.120
That's one kind of situation where RFRA could
be raised as a defense.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.809
It would not necessarily decide the outcome
of the case.

00:17:00.809 --> 00:17:03.769
The outcome of the case would depend on the
application of a legal test.

00:17:03.769 --> 00:17:09.480
Was there a compelling governmental interest
to override the pizza owner's conscience?

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:14.370
If there was, was it accomplished with the
least restrictive means?

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:19.780
Anyone who thinks that a case under that test
on those facts is a foregone conclusion as

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:24.300
to how it would come out, is not familiar
with the practice of law in state or federal

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.110
court in this country.

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:29.290
Legal tests do not provide automatic outcomes.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:31.490
They just don't.

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:38.040
On the other hand, when many people on the
Left really liked RFRAs, it involved such

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.250
more exotic religious practices such as smoking
peyote.

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:47.740
In fact, the right to smoke peyote in a religious
ritual was the motivation for the first and

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:54.630
grandest RFRA, the Supreme Court of the United
States struck down or reversed itself on its

00:17:54.630 --> 00:18:00.120
compelling governmental interest standard
when it received a case from a person who

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:04.630
sought the right to smoke peyote in a religious
ritual.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:08.960
The Supreme Court in a highly controversial
decision at the time said, "We're going to

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:09.960
change our test.

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:16.070
We're going to essentially say religious liberty
claim fails if it collides with a neutral

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:22.880
law of general applicability," which pretty
much swallowed the free exercise clause.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:29.350
There was a by-partisan reaction to that decision
that resulted in the National Religious Freedom

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:37.380
Restoration Act that reimposed that test originally
on both state and federal laws, but the Supreme

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:41.180
Court narrowed it down to just federal laws,
and that was what issued at the Hobby Lobby

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.360
cases.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:46.440
This was about a legal test.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:53.180
It was not about Jim Crow and it was not about
guaranteeing that a Christian baker or florist

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:57.680
could refuse to serve a gay wedding, because
not even the architects of the bill could

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:59.250
say that that's what would occur.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:03.180
That would have been up to a court.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:06.460
This was about much more than the facts.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:07.840
This was about symbols.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:17.850
It was about the symbolism of the sexual revolution,
possibly maybe, facing a small legal and political

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:19.180
reverse.

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:25.040
Small, because let's just face it, how many
bakers and florists have refused to serve

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:27.230
in gay weddings, or photographers?

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:30.560
I think I can count four to five.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:36.770
Last time I counted, I think the population
of this country is about 310, 315 million.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:43.720
To say that this would be a significant reverse
for sexual revolution, if four to five people

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:50.861
might could possibly win a case in refusing
to participate in a same sex wedding, that's

00:19:50.861 --> 00:19:52.290
your fantasy.

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:59.190
Symbols matter and symbols were important
and so the Battle of Indiana was launched.

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:07.610
Here is where I think the debate has begun
to change in my professional life.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:13.060
The first time I ever worked on a religious
liberty case was I was a 1L at Harvard Law

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:14.060
School.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:17.240
It was the fall of 1991.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:23.890
I was incredibly excited that a lawyer had
asked me to proofread a brief filed in a case,

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.850
and this is maybe the greatest case name ever.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:33.760
I can't even remember the plaintiff's name,
but the defendant was Hot, Sexy and Safer

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:36.440
Productions.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.400
The facts of the case were pretty amazing.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:44.190
In this Hot, Sexy and Safer case, students
were required to attend a student assembly

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:48.770
and at that student assembly, this was high
school, there were people engaged in simulated

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:55.720
sex acts on stage who urged students to experiment
sexually.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:59.040
They placed condoms onto bananas.

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:03.820
They did all kinds of things that you've heard
about in other cases and other places.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:08.070
Some Christian students objected to this and
one of them tried to stand up to leave.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:13.400
He was physically pushed down into his seat
by a school official.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:16.310
Physically pushed down and made to continue
to watch it.

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:21.280
They filed a claim and I thought, "Wow!

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:22.280
Outrageous!

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:28.270
America will rise up in anger at this violation
of individual liberty.

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.860
The courts will be outraged."

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:37.030
The case received approximately no press attention
and the student's case went down in flames,

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:41.190
lost at the trial court, lost in the first
circuit.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:45.700
To this day, as far as I know, it was a good
law you can require students to attend these

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:49.971
assemblies, witness these events that are
morally objectionably to large numbers in

00:21:49.971 --> 00:21:52.800
the students, and they may not leave.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:56.010
They're not constitutionally entitled to leave.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:59.270
Then I continued on in my practice.

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:03.240
In the year 2000, I had an interesting case
at Tufts University.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.890
This was one of my first cases when I had
left the work of the large law firm.

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:11.210
I was a lecturer at Cornell Law School at
the time, and a friend of a friend called

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:13.750
and said, "I have a problem at Tufts University."

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.340
"What's the problem?"

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:21.370
"My student group got thrown off campus in
an emergency meeting of the Student Conduct

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:22.370
Board."

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:23.370
"Why?"

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:30.210
"Well it turns out that we would not let an
openly lesbian woman become president of the

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:31.210
organization."

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:34.710
I thought, "You've been thrown out on an emergency
meeting.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:36.510
What was the emergency?"

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:43.550
Follow this logic, the Student Conduct Board
met and decided that Christianity teaches

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:45.130
there is such a thing as sin.

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:48.780
Are you following me so far?

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:54.110
A sense of sin leads to feelings of guilt.

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:57.130
Are you with me?

00:22:57.130 --> 00:23:02.990
Guilt leads to feelings of shame, including
deep shame.

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:08.220
Shame leads to ... Who wants to fill in the
last blank?

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.500
Suicide?

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:15.840
To protect the campus from suicide, the InterVarsity
Christian Fellowship at Tufts University had

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:19.210
to be thrown off campus post-haste.

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:21.550
I got involved in that case.

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:29.320
That case by at least what I thought as a
much younger lawyer, exploded in the media.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:31.650
I thought this is a turning point.

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:37.350
Now, Americans will realize there is a problem
that we're facing with the religious liberty.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:42.289
If a Christian student group cannot decide
that it would be led by a Christian.

00:23:42.289 --> 00:23:44.440
That's a problem.

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:46.440
Mainstream media covered it and PR covered
it.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:47.440
Everybody covered it.

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.900
I thought this is a fantastic turning point.

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:55.970
Eventually, the Student Conduct Board reversed
itself, allowed InterVarsity back on campus.

00:23:55.970 --> 00:24:00.180
In my naive younger days, I thought what a
tremendous victory.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:05.910
I was right, it was a victory, for the other
side because all of a sudden, campuses around

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:10.150
the country said, "I have an idea.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:14.030
Let's go on a search and destroy mission for
organizations like InterVarsity that have

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:15.310
similar policies."

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:20.540
My friend, Kim Colby, and I have been fighting
this for a very long time.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:25.210
We put together a list recently and these
are the cases that just Kim and I have been

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:33.290
involved in, and I stopped counting at 35
states and more than 100 individual institutions

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:36.830
where Christian student groups have either
been exclude from campus or threatened with

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:42.410
exclusion because they wanted leadership to
be Christian.

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:46.420
The battles kept going and I could go through
fact scenario after fact scenario after fact

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:51.840
scenario where again and again I would believe
if I pounded the table enough, if we yelled

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:58.270
loudly enough, if we put the right kind of
industry and attention into this issue, then

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:04.490
finally, the base of conservatism, the base
of Christian conservatism, the base of faithful

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:10.440
Christians would understand the stakes and
begin to respond.

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:16.590
Little did I know that going after campus
Christians wasn't the straw that would break

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.050
the camel's back.

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:22.080
Going after elementary school children wasn't
the straw that break the camel's back.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:26.460
Silencing chaplains wasn't the straw that
would break the camel's back.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.570
I can go on and on.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:35.320
The straw that would break the camel's back
is when they came for our chicken sandwiches.

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:42.360
When Chick-fil-A faced the wrath of the so-called
social justice warriors, all of a sudden the

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:47.380
sleeping giant awoke.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:51.090
I think that that was an important lesson
for those of us who deal with the public day

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:55.270
in, day out on these issues is that we're
really not that big a deal.

00:25:55.270 --> 00:26:01.260
We can have 15 million talking head appearances,
write 1,700 op-eds and talk about this day

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:09.110
in, day out, but there's nothing more powerful
than a good meal to really motivate people.

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:16.370
There was the largest anti-sexual revolution,
grassroots movement I've ever seen in my life

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:21.620
and may ever see when hundreds of Chick-fil-A
stores across the country sold out of their

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:22.620
chicken.

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:25.420
Never heard of a restaurant selling out of
its food.

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:31.280
My wife went to the "buycott" in our local
town and there was so much traffic that the

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:37.730
police officer was directing it with a big
Chick-fil-A cup in his hand to make sure that

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:43.390
there wasn't a traffic jam in Columbia, Tennessee,
which would have been the first ever non-meal

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:49.280
day related traffic jam in Columbia history,
and yes, we have a meal day.

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:53.610
Every local politician was there joining in,
"Me too, me too, I'm for Chick-fil-A."

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:56.990
It was an astonishing cultural moment.

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:03.640
It was also a valuable cultural moment because
it began to penetrate into the popular mind

00:27:03.640 --> 00:27:08.400
of the Christian community, the conservative
community, more broadly in this country that

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:11.970
there's something serious going on here.

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:17.910
That's why when I say "It's not gay rights
versus religious liberty.

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:22.880
It's donning on people this is sexual revolution
versus Christianity."

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:29.240
If you'll note every time there is a major
confrontation, pop culture confrontation on

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:34.270
these things, the Left narrows the issue.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:41.150
Wendy Davis filibusters and she is the queen
of defending the right to choose, then it's

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:47.890
all about this very narrow issue of the state
legislature intruding on women's body.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:53.870
Hobby Lobby, it's involved in a massive confrontation
leading to its own attempted and utterly failed

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:56.370
boycott from the Left against Hobby Lobby.

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.850
There it's not about a larger sweeping thing.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:02.660
It's about can women get contraceptives.

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:06.760
In Indiana, it's not about anything bigger
and sweeper, it's about this very specific

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:08.640
gay rights versus religious liberty.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.950
Are you going to mistreat this gay couple
who wants to get married?

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:14.660
Again and again, the focus is let's make this
small.

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:25.230
Why are you doing this discreet action that
hurts women or denies contraceptives or denies

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:29.410
a gay couple the pizza they so desperately
want at their reception?

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:33.559
This discreet action that the larger Christian
community is beginning to understand this

00:28:33.559 --> 00:28:34.940
is linked.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:36.400
This is absolutely linked.

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:42.330
It's about an overriding world view that is
not biblical.

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:43.510
It is anti-biblical.

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:53.360
That folding onto one element of it requires
by theological logic, folding on every element

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:54.650
of it.

00:28:54.650 --> 00:29:00.240
I would urge you guys to read in the Atlantic,
Ross Douthat has a piece about Pope Francis.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:04.440
He talks about how the Catholic Church is
facing right now this question about what

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:11.250
to do with couples who are divorced and remarried.

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:17.720
On the one side, there is an argument that
it's a very discreet decision that you can

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:23.309
grant communication to a remarried couple
without doing violence to the larger structure

00:29:23.309 --> 00:29:26.630
of Catholic theology on sexuality.

00:29:26.630 --> 00:29:32.251
On the other side is the argument that this
is part of a seamless hole, and that if you

00:29:32.251 --> 00:29:37.420
tear away one part of it, the theological
logic there is so relentless, you'll tear

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:38.740
away the rest.

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:47.320
That argument is winning the day in orthodox
denominations and churches around the country.

00:29:47.320 --> 00:29:53.800
That is why when you look at the Southern
Baptist Convention, when you look at Catholicism,

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:59.780
when you look at capital or orthodoxy, when
you look at evangelical non-denominational

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:04.550
churches, denominational churches around the
country, you're not seeing any movement on

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:05.550
this issue.

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:11.090
Instead, the argument is not how do we accommodate
the culture, the argument is what is the best

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:14.190
way to present our argument back to the culture.

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:19.200
I was just in a meeting with Russell Moore
at the Ethics &amp; Religious Liberty Commission

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:21.080
in Southern Baptist Convention.

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:24.461
As everyone knows, Southern Baptist Convention
is the largest Protestant denomination in

00:30:24.461 --> 00:30:31.140
the US, roughly 19 million plus people affiliated.

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:35.300
In question of the day wasn't how do we accommodate.

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:40.390
The question of the day is how do we persuade
about the truth of scripture.

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:46.240
When you have the largest denomination in
the United States, the Protestant denomination

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:53.190
focused on changing hearts and minds to its
position, it is not a time of gloom and doom.

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:55.360
Not a time of gloom and doom at all.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:31:04.090
That I think is leading directly into the
growth of the grassroots movement that exist

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:11.970
against totalitarianism, against conformity
in the sexual arena and the diminishment of

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:13.100
religious liberty.

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:21.230
I mentioned, of course, Chick-fil-A that's
legendary by this point, but let's also not

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:23.290
forget Hobby Lobby.

00:31:23.290 --> 00:31:30.170
Hobby Lobby was supposed to pay for its sin,
of paying for only 16 out of 20 contraceptives.

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:32.720
It was going to have to pay for its apostasy.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:34.770
It did not pay for its apostasy.

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:38.900
Instead, Hobby Lobby would not only won at
the Supreme Court of the United States, it

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:45.270
won on busily serving its customers who by
and large ... I think it won maybe second

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:51.040
or third largest store in my meal capital
of the world after Walmart is a Hobby Lobby.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:58.440
If you ask their customers, "Should these
folks whose store they patronize is full of

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:03.300
Christian symbols and imagery and Christian
books, including books by the founders of

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:09.300
the company that proclaim their Christian
faith, should they be forced to pay for pills

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.750
and devices that they believe cause abortion?"

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:14.720
They would say no, absolutely not.

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:18.920
I wouldn't want them to, so boycott utterly
failed.

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:23.810
I bring up this example and I bring it up
now because I agree with everything Phil Roberston

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:31.309
says or how it says it, but the effort to
drive a guy, I knew the Left is beginning

00:32:31.309 --> 00:32:38.020
to lose a bit of its cultural power when it
lost in an effort to drive a guy off its own

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:46.580
medium, television, a mainstream cable channel
for engaging in a speech against homosexual

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:51.790
activity that was pretty colorful and upfront.

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:54.020
They lost that battle.

00:32:54.020 --> 00:32:55.020
He's still there.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:59.760
He's still there to this day even though ... I
can't even open and read the Huffington Post

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:05.380
any given week without reading about a "fresh
outrage" from Phil Robertson, but he's still

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:06.380
there.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:07.620
They lost on that.

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:14.320
Think about this, in Texas, recently there
was an effort to subpoena the contents of

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:20.690
not just sermons but communications with parishioners,
between parishioners and pastors about sexual

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:21.790
issues.

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:23.600
Unbelievably overreaching.

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:25.460
Unbelievably overreaching.

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:29.960
Legal organizations stampeded in to file to
quash those subpoenas, but the public outrage

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:39.740
was so great that even a radical city government
in Houston capitulated maybe within 24 and

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:43.260
I think no later than within 48 hours.

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:49.100
Something is happening at the grassroots that
is very potent and very powerful.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:50.330
Let's not forget Indiana.

00:33:50.330 --> 00:34:02.540
In Indiana, two of the headlines were "Republicans
Cave", which sad to say ... Is that Tuesday?

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:07.910
Republicans Cave on multiple conflicts frequently,
as do Democrats.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:09.480
Compromise is part of politics.

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:15.359
The headlines were "Republicans Cave", "Memories
Pizza Closes Its Doors".

00:34:15.359 --> 00:34:21.399
This pizza company that had received death
threat, an avalanche, a negative Google and

00:34:21.399 --> 00:34:27.559
Yelp reviews closes its doors and then says,
"We don't know when we're going to be open,"

00:34:27.559 --> 00:34:32.349
lest well-covered in the media was what happened
next.

00:34:32.349 --> 00:34:39.659
They lashed and the folks of the Blaze initiate
a go fund me campaign, which for a small dollar

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:42.470
donations they helped reopen Memories Pizza.

00:34:42.470 --> 00:34:48.169
It raises, before they closed it down, $850,000
in less than a week.

00:34:48.169 --> 00:34:53.119
Now, some of you guys here are involved in
non-profit fundraising effort.

00:34:53.119 --> 00:35:01.690
I try raising $850,000 in small dollar donations
in less than a week without a pre-existing

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:07.640
mailing list, without a pre-existing affiliation
with the organization ... I mean, that an

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:08.700
astonishing accomplishment.

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:09.700
Astonishing.

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:12.460
They would have kept going had they not shut
it down.

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:16.130
Memories Pizza did what, it reopened to a
packed house.

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:23.849
While the headline was Politicians Cave, the
grassroots reality was Memories Pizza, this

00:35:23.849 --> 00:35:27.960
vilified organization prevails, and now they're
rich.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:32.730
I had no idea that small town pizza could
be so lucrative.

00:35:32.730 --> 00:35:35.940
The grassroots came through.

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:39.510
This brings back to reaffirming the last point
that I mentioned.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:45.099
As the grassroots comes through, the intellectual
core of the conservative Christianity, of

00:35:45.099 --> 00:35:48.069
conservatism more generally, remains intact.

00:35:48.069 --> 00:35:49.869
It might be despairing.

00:35:49.869 --> 00:35:57.210
In the Game of Thrones, winter is coming sort
of starkey and worldview, but it remains intact.

00:35:57.210 --> 00:36:02.029
As I mentioned earlier, Ross Douthat has been
writing invaluable stuff at New York Times.

00:36:02.029 --> 00:36:06.970
Many people in this room have been offering
their own contributions dismayed by the drift

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:12.100
of the culture but not yielding on the underlying
principles involved.

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:17.700
I would submit to you that history would show
that where the grassroots remains intact and

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:22.569
the intellectual core of a movement remain
intact, the politicians will follow.

00:36:22.569 --> 00:36:25.910
Rare is the politician who's a true leader.

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:30.390
They tend to be followers who follow their
culture; the culture that they come from.

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:33.759
A great example of this is the pro-life movement.

00:36:33.759 --> 00:36:39.470
For a very long time we'd had a real difficulty
making progress on life legislation, but in

00:36:39.470 --> 00:36:45.749
recent years, as the grassroots movement in
the states has grown in power, we have seen

00:36:45.749 --> 00:36:51.660
much of the abortion industry chagrin, more
pro-life legislation enacted say in the last

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:55.349
three years than the previous 10 combined.

00:36:55.349 --> 00:37:01.010
That's a result of politicians who are following
a culture that is energized on this issue,

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:06.569
and an intellectual core that also has always
been energized on this issue.

00:37:06.569 --> 00:37:11.279
Again, this sounds like I'm being optimistic.

00:37:11.279 --> 00:37:14.299
I'm just predicting stalemate.

00:37:14.299 --> 00:37:18.760
In predicting stalemate, that strikes some
people as wildly optimistic.

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:23.339
Here is where I think we can begin to turn
from stalemate into progress.

00:37:23.339 --> 00:37:27.619
As I mentioned earlier and then I'm going
to open this up for questions, the reality

00:37:27.619 --> 00:37:33.210
is particularly within the religious community,
that the argument, the sexual revolutionary

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:38.680
argument is failing, is failing to appeal
to people of faith.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:40.109
It just is.

00:37:40.109 --> 00:37:45.569
If you look at the denominations that have
opened their doors, who have proclaimed that

00:37:45.569 --> 00:37:52.960
they have opened their theology to a variety
of beliefs and practices that defy orthodox

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:56.220
Christianity, they're vanishing, they are.

00:37:56.220 --> 00:38:01.741
PCUSA, I just mentioned them not because of
any long-standing grudge since I'm part of

00:38:01.741 --> 00:38:09.410
the PCA and we were split off from them, but
if you compare the growth of the PCA with

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:15.839
the losses of the PCUSA, which last year loss
5% of its total membership and more than 100

00:38:15.839 --> 00:38:21.829
congregations in one year, with the PCA, which
has had 10-fold increase in the last 20 years,

00:38:21.829 --> 00:38:25.170
you can see where the energy and momentum
is.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:26.460
The mainstream media would tell you otherwise.

00:38:26.460 --> 00:38:31.420
A closer than example of this that we've seen
in my own backyard in Tennessee.

00:38:31.420 --> 00:38:36.390
Not too far from where I live is a evangelical
church called Grace Point Church.

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:39.210
It's a church that a lot of musicians like
to attend.

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:43.970
It's Carrie Underwood's church if you remember
Carrie Underwood of American Idol fame and

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:46.540
Jesus Take the Wheel fame and many other great
songs.

00:38:46.540 --> 00:38:47.640
I think I have four of her albums.

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:49.109
I'm a Carrie Underwood fan.

00:38:49.109 --> 00:38:55.089
She attends this church and it's long been
known as a "gay friendly" evangelical church.

00:38:55.089 --> 00:39:01.000
About three months or so ago, it took the
plunge and became the first "big" evangelical

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:06.279
church in Nashville to say, "We're going to
recognize same sex marriages."

00:39:06.279 --> 00:39:07.359
This was covered in Time Magazine.

00:39:07.359 --> 00:39:09.029
It was a big event.

00:39:09.029 --> 00:39:11.460
This is the crack in the wall of evangelical
churches.

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:13.210
It's a mega church in Nashville.

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:18.180
Now, for less than 1,000 members, that is
not a mega church in Nashville.

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:20.980
I can show you a mega church in Nashville.

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:25.050
City blocks of just churches.

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:29.340
At the very end of the article in Time, it
noticed that they had already lost almost

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:35.410
50% of their members in two weeks time after
announcing they're embracing same sex marriage,

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:38.200
and almost 50% of donations.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:41.680
Just two weeks ago, the church, what used
to have two and sometimes three services on

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:47.420
Sunday morning, launched just one service
called "Together in This", signaling I think

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:52.710
that they now believe that it's them against
the world, that so for Christian organizations,

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:56.380
recent history is proving the path to cultural
accommodation is the path to extinction.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:40:03.359
That if you look at the list of growing or
holding firm denominations in entities in

00:40:03.359 --> 00:40:09.130
the United States, it is those who maintained
adherence to orthodox Christianity.

00:40:09.130 --> 00:40:12.720
Granite, by definition, is strong.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:15.590
Limestone, by definition, is much weaker.

00:40:15.590 --> 00:40:22.160
We have born down to the granite and now it
is time to regather our strength and try to

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:25.349
make headway again back into the limestone.

00:40:25.349 --> 00:40:30.559
With that, I'm happy to take your questions.

00:40:30.559 --> 00:40:38.509
Speaker 4: Hello.

00:40:38.509 --> 00:40:42.839
My question for you is on the very nature
of religious liberty.

00:40:42.839 --> 00:40:45.339
I'm not entirely convinced that's the right
argument.

00:40:45.339 --> 00:40:47.910
I'm just wondering if you could fill this
out.

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.420
There's almost an assumption of total state
power to regulate private contracts that the

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:56.499
only way you can get out of that is by using
sincerely how the religious believe.

00:40:56.499 --> 00:41:02.510
In my mind, have we already lost the battle
by accepting this dichotomy that the government

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:03.510
really does.

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:06.119
It occupies the public square and the private
square.

00:41:06.119 --> 00:41:07.309
There is no distinction.

00:41:07.309 --> 00:41:09.710
The only way to get out of it is through religious
belief.

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:11.309
I'm just wondering if you could comment on
that.

00:41:11.309 --> 00:41:18.200
David French: I think that the religious liberty
argument is a nice segue into a larger discussion

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:21.500
of who owns a business.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:26.240
One of the things that struck me about the
Hobby Lobby argument was that in fact, just

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:29.930
what you said, the only real argument that
they had available to them was the religious

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:30.990
liberty argument under RFRA.

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:35.190
That was the only viable legal argument, certainly,
and the one that prevailed at the Supreme

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:42.050
Court, but it led to a larger conversation
that needs to occur about who really owns

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:43.440
the business.

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:47.369
We have reached the point where with through
voluminous economic regulations, health and

00:41:47.369 --> 00:41:52.080
safety regulations, public accommodation statutes,
that there is almost this assumption that

00:41:52.080 --> 00:42:01.210
people have that if I'm in a for-profit business,
I have essentially no rights if my desire

00:42:01.210 --> 00:42:05.200
to run my business in a particular manner
conflicts with the government's desire to

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:07.839
regulate me.

00:42:07.839 --> 00:42:13.910
I think the Hobby Lobby case was a cultural
moment to begin questioning that premise,

00:42:13.910 --> 00:42:14.910
but I would agree with you.

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:22.749
I think that it's quite distressing from a
liberty standpoint that the only real viable

00:42:22.749 --> 00:42:27.390
argument left to the Hobby Lobby owners was
that religious liberty argument, and had it

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:32.739
not been for RFRA, they would have had a very
difficult time in court.

00:42:32.739 --> 00:42:33.930
Yes.

00:42:33.930 --> 00:42:40.551
Speaker 5: I'd like to invite your comments
on the Benedict Option and specifically, I'd

00:42:40.551 --> 00:42:48.319
be interested in which legislative protection
do you think would need to be enacted first?

00:42:48.319 --> 00:42:53.269
David French: Let me describe to you what
I think the Benedict Option is because it's

00:42:53.269 --> 00:42:56.299
a new concept.

00:42:56.299 --> 00:43:02.150
The Benedict Option as I understand it is
a strategic withdrawal from pop culture, culture

00:43:02.150 --> 00:43:10.270
at large, retreating into your specific church
organizations, retrenching and reteaching

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:13.440
the truth of scripture and the church.

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:20.849
Then once you have resolidified at an indefinite
future date, you're going to sally back forth

00:43:20.849 --> 00:43:22.400
into the culture.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:26.099
Is that a fair description?

00:43:26.099 --> 00:43:31.150
I have two problems with that.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:39.150
One, tactical and one, legal or it's tactical/strategic,
also with theological elements thrown in.

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:46.789
Number one, teaching properly within churches,
the eternal truths of Christianity is something

00:43:46.789 --> 00:43:49.989
that should be the baseline of what we're
doing already.

00:43:49.989 --> 00:43:56.010
I think it's a bit of an exaggeration to believe
that we're not given the solidity of the various

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:58.509
major denomination on these core issues.

00:43:58.509 --> 00:44:03.930
I think that's already occurring to a greater
degree than many may perceive.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:10.140
Second things is if you begin to consciously
adopt that Benedict Option and retreating

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:15.549
back to the church proper, you're inadvertently
beginning to play into an argument that the

00:44:15.549 --> 00:44:19.130
Obama Administration and other Left had been
making is that religious liberty is the thing

00:44:19.130 --> 00:44:21.479
you do in church.

00:44:21.479 --> 00:44:26.839
If you retreat back to church, you're giving
a cultural argument that yes, in fact, that

00:44:26.839 --> 00:44:28.390
is where religious liberty occurs.

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:30.950
It is in church.

00:44:30.950 --> 00:44:36.900
One of the, I think more subtle and damaging
aspects of the entire debate over the HHS

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:41.740
mandate, was the extent to which well if you
are in a church plan, with a church organization,

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:42.950
you were fine.

00:44:42.950 --> 00:44:44.980
Move along, nothing to see here.

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:51.160
All part of this effort just redefined religious
liberty as the "right to worship" when religious

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:53.680
liberty is really something much more broad.

00:44:53.680 --> 00:45:00.049
Finally, I think that the Benedict Option
in my mind is inconsistent with the call to

00:45:00.049 --> 00:45:02.119
be salt and light.

00:45:02.119 --> 00:45:03.970
There's many different ways that you can do
that.

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:07.650
There are ways subtle, ways that are overt.

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:15.510
One of the more fun and I used that term sarcastically,
debates in the larger Christian community

00:45:15.510 --> 00:45:16.510
is over.

00:45:16.510 --> 00:45:20.019
Which forms of Christians art that they're
really good forms of Christian art to sally

00:45:20.019 --> 00:45:21.140
forth into the culture?

00:45:21.140 --> 00:45:28.009
You have on one side, the people are saying
that the very explicit religious communication

00:45:28.009 --> 00:45:31.309
like say, God's not Dead, that's the way to
go.

00:45:31.309 --> 00:45:32.660
Others say, "No, no, no, no.

00:45:32.660 --> 00:45:34.960
That's just heavy-handed and nobody likes
it.

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:36.759
It's like 3% in the Rotten Tomatoes.

00:45:36.759 --> 00:45:41.989
We're going to go with the much more subtle,
cool, hipster way to do it."

00:45:41.989 --> 00:45:44.930
I'm of the mindset of do it all.

00:45:44.930 --> 00:45:45.950
Do it all.

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:49.020
Be direct, be subtle, but be salt and light.

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:52.359
I feel like the Benedict Option and I don't
think Rod would say he doesn't want to be

00:45:52.359 --> 00:45:57.720
salt and light, but I think that one of the
implications of that is a retreat is inconsistent

00:45:57.720 --> 00:45:59.970
with that salt and light imperative.

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:00.970
Yes.

